ry code for the ethical use of AI. Along with Singapore, other governments and mega-corporations are beginning to establish their own guidelines. Britain is setting up a data ethics center. India released its AI ethics strategy this spring.\n\nOn June 7 Google pledged not to “design or deploy AI” that would cause “overall harm,” or to develop AI-directed weapons or use AI for surveillance that would violate international norms. It also pledged not to deploy AI whose use would violate international laws or human rights.\n\nWhile the statement is vague, it represents one starting point. So does the idea that decisions made by AI systems should be explainable, transparent, and fair.\n\nTo put it another way: How can we make sure that the thinking of intelligent machines reflects humanity’s highest values? Only then will they be useful servants and not Frankenstein’s out-of-control monster.","paragraphs":[{"id":"3834da12-a009-4885-af9e-d3de6a0807ef","sentences":[{"id":"bd62ee50-3214-45fe-a32f-f91489cd282e","text":"This year marks exactly two centuries since the publication of “Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus,” by Mary Shelley."},{"id":"9b6c8377-e905-4f1f-a48e-7831a6fc66a9","text":"Even before the invention of the electric light bulb, the author produced a remarkable work of speculative fiction that would foreshadow many ethical questions to be raised by technologies yet to come."}]},{"id":"0abbb982-1839-4649-ae99-9e41db555566","sentences":[{"id":"0a413eb6-6a0f-4ad6-ab12-adb290d7bde7","text":"Today the rapid growth of artificial intelligence (AI) raises fundamental questions: “What is intelligence, identity, or consciousness?"},{"id":"c06a5acb-d3f5-4481-a08e-77a5635d3737","text":"What makes humans humans?”"}]},{"id":"aee9ce31-73ff-49bb-a627-cbd004996ca8","sentences":[{"id":"33005e6d-9dd5-4524-aea2-547f7be561cd","text":"What is being called artificial general intelligence, machines that would imitate the way humans think, continues to evade scientists."},{"id":"70